content.text:
Any improvement can be made from this below ideation?



They are typed — just inferred, not annotated. SearchFn already has the matching types; this file does not import them.

`nucleumDatafnSearchDefaults` is a `SearchDefaults` from `@searchfn/adapter-contracts`:
```85:89:/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev/searchfn/adapter-contracts/src/index.ts
export interface SearchDefaults {
  fuzzy?: boolean | number;
  prefix?: boolean;
  fieldBoosts?: Record<string, number>;
}
```

`nucleumDatafnSearchPipeline` is a `PipelineOptions` from `@searchfn/core`:
```31:41:/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev/searchfn/core/src/pipeline/types.ts
export interface PipelineOptions {
  enableStemming?: boolean;
  // ...
  enableEdgeNGrams?: boolean;
  edgeNGramMinLength?: number;
  edgeNGramMaxLength?: number;
  edgeNGramFieldConfig?: Record<string, EdgeNGramFieldConfig>;
}
```

`@21n/shared-data` only depends on `@datafn/core`, so this module cannot name those types without adding a SearchFn dependency. Call sites already check the shape: IndexedDB and OpenSearch adapters take `defaults` / `pipeline` on construct.

`as const` is doing two things instead of an annotation:

- Keeps literals (`prefix: true`, `fuzzy: 1`, boosts as exact numbers) for the index-version hash.
- Does **not** check the object against SearchFn. A typo like `prefx` or `enableSteming` would still compile here and only fail later when passed into an adapter.

If you want both, `satisfies SearchDefaults` / `satisfies PipelineOptions` is the fit: still `as const`-narrow, but validated. That would mean a type-only SearchFn import on this package, which it currently avoids.

