content.text:
Yes. The recommendation is right, but the discussion misses two important TypeScript details and one larger live inconsistency.

- `as const` does not affect the index-version hash. The hash receives the same runtime values either way. It only provides compile-time literal narrowing and `readonly` properties.
- “It will fail later at the adapter” is too optimistic. Because these are variables and SearchFn’s properties are optional, structural typing allows misspelled extra properties. I verified that `enableSteming` alongside valid properties passes when supplied to `PipelineOptions`; the adapter simply ignores it. `satisfies` at declaration is therefore the correct safeguard.
- Prefer the canonical combined contract instead of importing two separate types:

```ts
import type { SearchAdapterConfig } from "@searchfn/adapter-contracts";

type SearchDefaultsConfig =
  NonNullable<SearchAdapterConfig["defaults"]>;

type SearchPipelineConfig =
  NonNullable<SearchAdapterConfig["pipeline"]>;

export const nucleumDatafnSearchDefaults = {
  // ...
} as const satisfies SearchDefaultsConfig;

export const nucleumDatafnSearchPipeline = {
  // ...
} as const satisfies SearchPipelineConfig;
```

That requires one explicit type-level SearchFn dependency and adds no runtime import. If `@21n/shared-data` must remain strictly DataFn-only, move this module to a SearchFn-aware shared package instead of duplicating the interfaces locally.

There are two more substantive issues worth adding to the ideation:

1. `satisfies` still won’t validate field names.

Both `fieldBoosts` and `edgeNGramFieldConfig` use `Record<string, ...>`, so `titel` would still compile. Currently, `title` is configured in both places at [search.provider.ts](/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus/shared/data/datafn/search.provider.ts:9), but no schema-derived search index contains a `title` field. It is currently dead configuration. A schema-aware field union or runtime invariant would catch that.

2. The search disclosure has already drifted.

